  <div class="mt-8 space-y-8 text-body dark:text-muted">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
      Malaysian cross-border commuters traveling daily across the Causeway and Indian professionals holding Employment Passes (EP) represent two of Singapore's most vital and robust foreign talent communities. However, navigating <strong>Singapore income tax for Malaysians and Indian expats</strong> is not just a straightforward matter of looking up domestic tax rates. It requires a profound understanding of bilateral <a href="https://www.iras.gov.sg/taxes/international-tax/double-taxation-agreements-(dtas)" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline">Double Taxation Agreements (DTAs)</a>, Tax Residency Certificates (TRC), CPF exemption rules, and foreign remittance tax laws.
    </p>

    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed font-sans">
      Whether you are an engineer from Penang, a tech lead from Bengaluru, or an executive relocating from London, cross-border taxation brings its own unique set of anxieties. The fear of double taxation, compliance missteps, and penalties from either the Inland Revenue Authority of Singapore (IRAS), the Lembaga Hasil Dalam Negeri (LHDN) in Malaysia, or the Income Tax Department in India can weigh heavily on expats. This comprehensive 1,500+ word guide is designed to dissect these complexities and offer practical, empathetic, and expert-backed guidance to ensure your wealth is protected and fully compliant across jurisdictions.
    </p>

